window.docContents[123].resolve({"/Colorings/#section-degree-less-than":{"contents":"In order to define the set of neighbours of a vertex v that are less than v, we\nneed some kind of ordering on the vertices. The simplest form this can take is an\ninstance of LT on the vertex type.\n\nThe set of neighbours of v that are less than v.section degreeLT\nvariable {V : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph V) [LT V]\n\n/-- The set of neighbours of v that are less than v. -/\ndef neighborLTSet (v : V) : Set V := {u | u < v ∧ G.Adj v u}\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mem_neighborLTSet (v : V) (u : V) :\n    u ∈ G.neighborLTSet v ↔ u < v ∧ G.Adj v u := Iff.rfl\n\n\nThe assumption that this set is finite is Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v).\nWith this finiteness assumption we can define the Finset version of this set.\n\nThe neighbours of v that are less than v as a Finset./-- The neighbours of v that are less than v as a Finset. -/\ndef neighborLTFinset (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] : Finset V :=\n  (G.neighborLTSet v).toFinset\n\n\n@[simp]\nlemma mem_neighborLTFinset (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] (u : V) :\n    u ∈ G.neighborLTFinset v ↔ u < v ∧ G.Adj v u := Set.mem_toFinset\n\n\nFinally the degreeLT is just the size of this finite set.\n\nThe number of neighbours of v that are less than v./-- The number of neighbours of v that are less than v. -/\ndef degreeLT (v : V) [Fintype (G.neighborLTSet v)] : ℕ :=\n  (G.neighborLTFinset v).card\n\nend degreeLT\n\n\nRecall from Finite Neighbourhoods that G.LocallyFinite is an\nabbreviation for ∀ v, Fintype (G.neighborSet v): the assumption that every vertex\nin a graph G has finitely many neighbours. We now need its LT analogue: every\nvertex in G has finitely many earlier neighbours.\n\nabbrev LocallyLTFinite {V : Type*} [LT V] (G : SimpleGraph V) :=\n  ∀ n, Fintype (G.neighborLTSet n)\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Colorings\u0009Greedy Colouring*","header":"7.8.1. Degree-less-than","id":"/Colorings/#section-degree-less-than"},"/Constructions/#section-line-graphs":{"contents":"Given G : SimpleGraph V, its line graph is the graph whose vertices are the edges\nof G, in which two distinct edges are adjacent iff they share a vertex. In Mathlib\nthis is defined as follows.\n\n#source lineGraph -- def lineGraph : SimpleGraph G.edgeSet where\n                  --    Adj e₁ e₂ := e₁ ≠ e₂ ∧ (e₁ ∩ e₂ : Set V).Nonempty\n                  --    symm.symm e₁ e₂ hadj := by rwa [ne_comm, Set.inter_comm]\n\n\nWe introduce the notation L[G] for the line graph of G.\n\n@[inherit_doc lineGraph]\nscoped notation \"L[\" G \"]\" => lineGraph G\n\n\nThe edge set G.edgeSet is a Set (Sym2 V), so to use it as the vertex type of\nL[G], Lean coerces it to the subtype {e : Sym2 V // e ∈ G.edgeSet}, written\n↑G.edgeSet. A vertex of L[G] is therefore an edge bundled with a proof that it\nbelongs to G: a term ⟨s(u, v), h⟩ with h : s(u, v) ∈ G.edgeSet. This membership\nis the same as G.Adj u v (mem_edgeSet). For a concrete graph, decide can supply\nh.\n\n#check L[K[4]] -- SimpleGraph ↑K[4].edgeSet\n#check (⟨s(0, 1), by decide⟩ : K[4].edgeSet)\n\n\nAdjacency in L[G] has two parts. The condition e₁ ≠ e₂ is needed because every\nedge shares a vertex with itself, and simple graphs have no loops. The condition\n(e₁ ∩ e₂ : Set V).Nonempty says that the edges share a vertex. To state it, each\nedge is coerced twice: first from ↑G.edgeSet to Sym2 V, and then from Sym2 V to\nSet V, the set of its endpoints.\n\nFor example, s(0, 1) and s(0, 2) are adjacent in L[K[4]]. After\nconstructor, the first goal says that they are distinct and the second that they\nshare a vertex, for which 0 is a witness.\n\nLeft: K_4. Right: its line graph, the octahedron\n\nexample : L[K[4]].Adj ⟨s(0, 1), by decide⟩ ⟨s(0, 2), by decide⟩ := by\n  constructor\n  · grind\n  · -- After the double coercion we need to check that the Set (Fin 4)\n    -- {0, 1} and {0, 2} have nonempty intersection.\n    -- ⊢ (↑↑⟨s(0, 1), ⋯⟩ ∩ ↑↑⟨s(0, 2), ⋯⟩).Nonempty\n    use 0; simp\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions\u0009Operations on Graphs","header":"3.3.1. Line Graphs","id":"/Constructions/#section-line-graphs"}});